{
  "summary": "This computer looks in good shape: the scan found only three minor items and nothing urgent. Two are changes since the scan 7 days ago that look like normal software updates, and one is a reminder to confirm you recognize an administrator account. The most useful thing to do first is simply check that you know the <account-2> account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize the <account-2> administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account called <computer>\\<account-2> with full administrator rights, meaning it can change anything on the computer. Having a separate admin account is actually good practice, and this one was not newly added since the last scan (new admins would have been reported at high). It's still worth a quick look: if you or someone in your household set it up, there's nothing to worry about. If you don't recognize it, treat that seriously and see the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Microsoft Defender's program location changed (looks like an update)",
      "finding_ids": [
        "CHANGED-PERSISTENCE:47c86358"
      ],
      "explanation": "The WinDefend service, which is Microsoft Defender antivirus, now starts its program from a slightly different folder than a week ago. The only change is the version number in the folder name (4.18.25010-1 to 4.18.25020-2), and both paths sit in Defender's usual Platform folder. This is what a routine Defender update typically looks like. The rule flags such changes because attackers sometimes redirect trusted entries, but nothing here points that way."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "New Google updater task",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-PERSISTENCE:c74ac833"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task named \\GoogleUpdaterTaskSystem appeared since the last scan. It runs Google's updater from the Program Files (x86)\\Google folder, which is normal when Google software such as Chrome is installed or updates itself. It's listed for information only. If you don't use any Google software, the report shows how to look into it further."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
